A||N||6||fierovette||location cf oil pressure sender?||||5||
Z||000000||fierovette||06-22-2001||08:02 AM||||Trying to locate oil pressure sending unit, gage pegs needle at max, was told to replace sending unit, where is it located?||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||06-22-2001||08:15 AM||||welcome to the forum.. look to the left of the battery.. it's a smal black plastic can with a 3 wire lead running to it. don't twist the housing.. you'll need a 17mm wrench to grab the nut on the bottom of the unit... if you try and turn the housing you'll just wipe out the plug.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fsthydrantsig.jpg">         <BR>www.engineered.net||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||06-22-2001||10:04 AM||||Pictures are worth a thousand words, I posted links to pics on the thread you started in General Fiero Chat:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008295.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008295.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steven||06-22-2001||10:36 AM||||Now those pics are for the 87-88 Fiero. If you have an 85-86, it is threaded in to the block right next to the oil filter. So if you lay on your back under the Fiero with you feet coming the same direction as the tail pipes. look up past the converter to see the oil filter. To the left of the filter you will see a little can threaded in there. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||06-22-2001||02:45 PM||||Uh, nope.<P>As I mentioned in the other thread, those pictures are of my '86 SE, equipped with factory A/C, with about 28K miles on it at the time. One of those pictures clearly shows the plenum isn't engraved, but has the '85-'86 sticker.<P>The differentiator is if the car was or wasn't equipped with factory A/C.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steven||06-22-2001||02:50 PM||||Sorry Larry, thanks for the correction. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT-maniac||06-22-2001||07:41 PM||||As the owner of an 85 without A/C I can confirm that the sender is where steven says. On the block next to the filter. I'd recomemmend getting the  tube that the newer cars had that relocates the sender up higher if you don't have it. The relocation makes it easier to change the filter and better protects this very expensive sender from damage. You will have to wire in a new sensor socket or extend your old one as the stock wiring is not long enough to reach the new sensor location.<p>[This message has been edited by GT-maniac (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
